---
/*  What Reverb adds to the window every plugin shares: its room, its send and return, and the reflections it
    draws for the probe. The pictures are Reverb's own, drawn by its picture tool. */
import TutorialPage from '../../layouts/TutorialPage.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const img = (name: string) => `${base}/tutorial/${name}.webp`;
const window = `${base}/tutorial/the-window`;

const parts: [string, string][] = [
  ['room', 'the room'],
  ['send-return', 'send and return'],
  ['scene', 'the 3D view'],
  ['try', 'things to try'],
];
---

<TutorialPage
  id="reverb"
  sub="one room around the whole sound scene: early reflections and reverb tail"
  parts={parts}
  window={{ src: img('reverb-window'), alt: 'A reverb window: the 3D view with the send and return regions and the reflections, the matrix, and the send tab.', caption: 'a reverb window' }}
>
  <div class="prose" slot="intro">
    <p class="lede">
      Reverb puts an ambisonic signal in a room: early reflections from the room's walls, each arriving from its
      own direction, and a diffuse reverb tail after them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its window is the one every bambi plugin has; the 3D view, the matrix, presets and the link are explained in
      <a href={window}>the window</a>. This page covers what is Reverb's own: its three tabs, <code>room</code>,
      <code>send</code> and <code>return</code>, and what it shows in the 3D view.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="room" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('reverb-room')} alt="The room tab: six rooms with hall chosen, size, decay, tone, roughness, distance, pre-delay, and the input's low and high cut." width="696" height="830" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>the room</h2>
      <p>
        The row at the top is six rooms to start from: <code>ambience</code>, <code>room</code>,
        <code>chamber</code>, <code>hall</code>, <code>large hall</code> and <code>cathedral</code>. Choosing one
        sets the room's settings except pre-delay, and the input filters; it leaves pre-delay, the levels, the
        quality and the regions as they are. The room stays highlighted
        while you edit its settings, and a <strong>double-click</strong> restores them.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>size</code></strong>, 3 to 40 metres: when the reflections arrive and how dense they are.</li>
        <li><strong><code>decay</code></strong>, 0.2 to 10 seconds: how long the reverb tail lasts.</li>
        <li><strong><code>tone</code></strong>: how much faster the highs decay than the lows.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>roughness</code></strong>: how much the walls diffuse the sound. Smooth walls give clear mirror
          reflections; rough ones send more into the tail.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>distance</code></strong>, 0.5 to 20 metres: how far away the sound seems, which comes
          across mostly as the balance of dry signal and reverb. It is one value for the whole input, since a
          reverb on a bus cannot know each source's distance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>pre-delay</code></strong>, up to 200 milliseconds: delays the reverb tail further, on top of
          where it already starts. The early reflections are not moved.
        </li>
        <li><strong>input</strong>: a <code>low cut</code> and a <code>high cut</code> on the signal going into the reverb.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="send-return" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('reverb-send')} alt="The send tab: sectors chosen, four of them, their fill and softness." width="696" height="780" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>send and return</h2>
      <p>Reverb has two regions, each on its own tab:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>send</code></strong> is the part of the sound scene that goes into the reverb.</li>
        <li><strong><code>return</code></strong> is where the reverb comes back from.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both are <code>everywhere</code> to start with. Narrow the send, and only sound arriving from there goes
        into the reverb; narrow the return, and the reverb comes only from there. The dry signal still carries the
        whole sound scene. Shaping and moving a region works the same in every plugin: see
        <a href={`${window}#regions`}>regions</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scene">
    <h2>the 3D view</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('reverb-scene')} alt="The 3D view with the probe placed: marks in Reverb's colour where the reflections arrive, inside the send and return regions." width="1236" height="602" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Place the <a href={`${window}#scene`}>probe</a> in the 3D view and Reverb shows the early reflections a
        sound from there would get: a mark for each, where it arrives from and how strong it is. Only the mirror
        reflections are drawn. What the walls diffuse goes into the tail, which comes from everywhere and has no
        direction to draw. The display is computed from the plugin's current room, so it changes as you change
        the room.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>things to try</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>A bigger room.</strong> Place the probe, choose <code>hall</code>, then <code>large hall</code>:
          the marks spread further apart and the reverb tail gets longer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Clear walls, then rough ones.</strong> Turn <code>roughness</code> down and the reflections stand
          out as echoes off the walls; turn it up and they shrink as more of the sound goes into the tail.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A room behind you.</strong> In <code>return</code>, choose a spot and put it behind the
          listener. Whatever goes in, the reverb comes from behind.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</TutorialPage>
